---
import GenericPage from '../components/GenericPage.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import { fetchPredictions } from '../utils/predictions.js';

const baseUrl = /** @type {string} */ ((import.meta as any).env?.BASE_URL) || '/';
const predictions = fetchPredictions();

const methodology = predictions?.methodology ?? null;
const ex = methodology?.example ?? null;
const scoredWeeks = methodology?.scored_weeks ?? null;
const weekLabel = predictions?.predictedWeek?.label ?? null;

const round1 = (v: number | null | undefined) =>
	v == null ? null : Math.round(v * 10) / 10;
// slot_hit_rate is 0-1; likelihood_pct is already 0-100.
const slotRatePct = ex ? round1(ex.slot_hit_rate * 100) : null;

// Small plain-language ordinal for the overdue position (1st, 2nd, ...).
const ordinal = (n: number) => {
	const s = ['th', 'st', 'nd', 'rd'];
	const v = n % 100;
	return n + (s[(v - 20) % 10] || s[v] || s[0]);
};
const rankOrdinal = ex ? ordinal(ex.overdue_rank) : null;
---

<GenericPage
	title="Methodology - WRF Discounts"
	description="How the WRFrontiers discount predictions work, in plain terms: line robots up by how overdue they are, then show how often each spot has paid off in the past."
	baseUrl={baseUrl}
>
	<NavBar baseUrl={baseUrl} currentPage="predictions" />
	<h1>How predictions are calculated</h1>
	<div class="method-content">
		<p class="intro">
			The <a href={`${baseUrl}predictions`}>Predictions</a> page is our best guess
			at which robots and titans get discounted next &mdash; not an official
			announcement, just a read of how discounts have gone in the past. Here is the
			whole idea in two simple steps.
		</p>

		<section>
			<h2>Step 1 &mdash; Line them up by how overdue they are</h2>
			<p>
				Robots tend to come back around for a discount on a rough cycle. So we line
				every robot up by one thing: <strong>how long it has been since its last
				discount</strong>. The robot that has waited longest goes to the front of
				the line, the next-longest behind it, and so on. The ones near the front are
				"due".
			</p>
			<p class="fine">
				A robot needs at least <strong>2 past discounts</strong> before we include
				it, so we have some idea of its cycle. That mostly leaves out the
				<strong>newest robots</strong> &mdash; and there is an honest catch to that:
				a brand-new robot can still swoop in and grab a discount slot, bumping one of
				the robots we listed. We have no cycle to judge a newcomer by, so we leave it
				out rather than guess. The practical effect is that each listed robot's real
				chance is <strong>a little lower</strong> than the number shown.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Step 2 &mdash; How likely is each one?</h2>
			<p>
				Being at the front of the line is not a guarantee, so we put a real number
				on it &mdash; and that number comes straight from the track record of each
				spot in the line.
			</p>
			<p>
				We look back over every past week and ask a simple question: when a robot was
				sitting in <em>this</em> spot in the overdue line, how often did it actually
				get discounted? If a spot came through in, say, 24 of the last 45 weeks,
				that is about a 53&percnt; chance &mdash; so that is the number we show for
				whoever is in that spot now.
			</p>
			<p class="fine">
				Because it is just counting what really happened, the percentage stays honest
				on its own, and it updates as new weeks are added. One surprise falls out of
				it: the robot that has waited the <em>longest</em> is not always the most
				likely &mdash; a very long dry spell often means a robot that keeps getting
				skipped. That is why the cards are ordered by the percentage, not by the
				wait. The example below shows exactly that.
			</p>
		</section>

		{ex && (
			<section class="example">
				<h2>Worked example{weekLabel ? <> &mdash; {weekLabel}</> : null}</h2>
				<p class="disclaimer">
					These figures are this week's actual numbers, shown only to make the
					method concrete. <strong>They change every week</strong> as the history
					grows and the predicted week moves &mdash; do not read them as fixed
					constants.
				</p>
				<ol class="walk">
					<li>
						<strong>{ex.name}</strong> was last discounted{' '}
						<strong>{ex.weeks_since_discount}</strong> weeks ago &mdash; the{' '}
						<strong>{rankOrdinal}</strong> longest wait in the line right now
						(Step&nbsp;1).
					</li>
					<li>
						Looking back over the last <strong>{scoredWeeks}</strong> weeks, whoever
						sat in that <strong>{rankOrdinal}</strong> spot ended up discounted in{' '}
						<strong>{ex.slot_hits}</strong> of them (Step&nbsp;2).
					</li>
					<li>
						<div class="formula formula--calc">
							{ex.slot_hits} out of {scoredWeeks} = {slotRatePct}&percnt;
						</div>
						So <strong>{ex.name}</strong> is shown at{' '}
						<strong>{round1(ex.likelihood_pct)}&percnt;</strong> &mdash; the number
						on its card on the Predictions page.
					</li>
				</ol>
			</section>
		)}

		<section>
			<h2>What about titans?</h2>
			<p>
				Titans work the same way, with one twist: they are only discounted some
				weeks, not every week. So instead of "the chance this titan is discounted
				next week", their percentage answers "<em>when</em> a titan does get
				discounted, the chance it is this one." The{' '}
				<a href={`${baseUrl}predictions`}>Predictions page</a> also tells you how
				often no titan is discounted at all.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Does it actually work?</h2>
			<p>
				We keep ourselves honest: every past week's prediction is saved exactly as
				it was shown at the time, then checked against what was really discounted.
				You can see the full week-by-week record, and how often we get it right, on
				the <a href={`${baseUrl}history`}>Prediction History</a> page.
			</p>
		</section>

		<p class="back">
			<a href={`${baseUrl}predictions`}>&larr; Back to predictions</a>
		</p>
	</div>
</GenericPage>

<style>
	.method-content {
		max-width: 820px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	.intro {
		color: var(--text-color);
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	section {
		margin-bottom: 2.25rem;
		padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
	}

	section:last-of-type {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	h2 {
		color: var(--accent-color);
		margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
	}

	p {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
		color: var(--text-color);
	}

	.fine {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}

	a {
		color: var(--accent-color);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	.formula {
		font-family: 'Courier New', ui-monospace, monospace;
		background: var(--sidebar-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 8px;
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		color: var(--text-color);
		overflow-x: auto;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.formula--calc {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		color: var(--accent-color);
	}

	.example {
		background: var(--active-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 12px;
		padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
	}

	.disclaimer {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		font-style: italic;
	}

	.walk {
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding-left: 1.25rem;
	}

	.walk li {
		margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--text-color);
	}

	code {
		font-family: 'Courier New', ui-monospace, monospace;
		background: var(--sidebar-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
		font-size: 0.85em;
	}

	.back {
		margin-top: 2rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.method-content {
			padding: 0 0.5rem;
		}
	}
</style>
